## Claim
Text: "US President Trump opened a complaint platform to report censorship on Facebook, Twitter and YouTube."
Claim date: September 01, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did the administration of U.S. President Donald Trump formally launch or announce a complaint submission system (such as a website, form, or portal) in 2020?
Answer: The search result documents a complaint tool launched by the White House in May 2019, not in 2020. Therefore, the provided information is insufficient to answer the question about a formal launch or announcement in 2020.

Source URL: https://www.theverge.com/2019/5/15/18626785/white-house-trump-censorsip-tool-twitter-instagram-facebook-conservative-bias-social-media

### Was the primary stated purpose of the platform to collect public reports about online censorship by social media companies?
Answer: Yes, the primary stated purpose of the platform was to collect public reports about online censorship by social media companies. The White House explicitly tweeted, "No matter your views, if you suspect political bias has caused you to be censored or silenced online, we want to hear about it!" and directed users to a form to describe the alleged bias and the platform involved.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2019/05/15/tech/white-house-social-media-bias-tool/index.html

### Were Facebook, Twitter, and YouTube explicitly named as the target platforms for censorship complaints on this system?
Answer: Yes, Facebook, Twitter, and YouTube were explicitly named as the target platforms for the complaint system. According to the search result, "The tool will allow members of the public to report possible political censorship on Facebook, Twitter and Google's YouTube."

Source URL: https://abcnews.go.com/US/trump-ramps-war-political-bias-social-media-reporting/story?id=63067737

### Is there official documentation (e.g., a White House press release, executive order, or government blog post) that confirms President Trump authorized or publicized this complaint platform?
Answer: While the search result from Ars Technica describes the White House launching a tool for reporting social media censorship in May 2019 and includes details from the form itself, it does not link to or provide the text of any official White House press release, executive order, or government blog post. The source is a news report about the initiative, not the primary documentation itself. Therefore, the search result does not contain sufficient information to confirm the existence of such official documentation.

Source URL: https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/05/white-house-unveils-new-tool-to-report-censorship-by-social-media-giants/

### On what specific date was the complaint platform first made available to the public, and does it align with the claim date of September 1, 2020?
Answer: The complaint platform was first made available to the public on Wednesday, May 15, 2019, which does not align with the claim date of September 1, 2020. The source explicitly states, "On Wednesday, his White House launched a new online form," with the article dated May 2019.

Source URL: https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/05/white-house-unveils-new-tool-to-report-censorship-by-social-media-giants/

### Did any federal agency (e.g., the Federal Communications Commission, the Department of Justice) administer or oversee the platform, or was it managed by a different entity?
Answer: The platform was managed directly by the White House, not by a federal agency such as the FCC or DOJ. The search result states that "his White House launched a new online form" and the form itself asks users to "share your story with President Trump," indicating it was an initiative run from the White House.

Source URL: https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/05/white-house-unveils-new-tool-to-report-censorship-by-social-media-giants/

### Did representatives from Facebook, Twitter, or YouTube issue any public statement or response regarding this specific complaint platform?
Answer: Yes, a Twitter spokesperson issued a public statement regarding the complaint platform, saying, "We enforce the Twitter Rules impartially for all users, regardless of their background or political affiliation. We are constantly working to improve our systems and will continue to be transparent in our efforts." Facebook and YouTube did not issue a public response, as the source notes they "did not immediately respond to requests for comment."

Source URL: https://www.theverge.com/2019/5/15/18626785/white-house-trump-censorsip-tool-twitter-instagram-facebook-conservative-bias-social-media

## Final Judgement
The fact-check has confirmed that the White House under President Trump did indeed launch a complaint platform targeting Facebook, Twitter, and YouTube to collect reports of political censorship. However, the evidence consistently shows this platform was launched in May 2019, not in 2020 as the claim states.

The key missing information is any evidence of a similar platform being launched or reopened in 2020. All documented instances from the retrieved sources relate exclusively to the May 2019 launch.

Based on the recorded evidence, the claim's specific date is incorrect, which refutes the claim as stated. `refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The Trump White House launched an online form in May 2019—specifically on May 15, 2019—for the public to report perceived political censorship by social media platforms, explicitly including [Facebook, Twitter, and YouTube](https://abcnews.go.com/US/trump-ramps-war-political-bias-social-media-reporting/story?id=63067737). The tool was [directly managed by the White House](https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2019/05/white-house-unveils-new-tool-to-report-censorship-by-social-media-giants/), not a federal agency, and its launch drew a public [response from a Twitter spokesperson](https://www.theverge.com/2019/5/15/18626785/white-house-trump-censorsip-tool-twitter-instagram-facebook-conservative-bias-social-media). However, all evidence in the record confirms this initiative occurred in 2019, not in September 2020 as the claim states, and no information was found about a similar platform being launched or reopened in 2020.